# Java Quality Gates

## Purpose
Define a reusable verification contract for Java deliverables. The orchestrator and CI tooling may implement this contract independently.

## Gate stages

### JQG-001 · Metadata and applicability (MUST)
Resolve applicable corporate/departmental policies and project specifications, including versions. Validate `.iasi` metadata and resolve conflicting rules explicitly.
**Evidence:** resolved-policy inventory and validation report.

### JQG-002 · Build (MUST)
Build from a clean checkout using recorded toolchain and dependency versions. Fail the gate on compilation or packaging errors.
**Evidence:** build logs and artifact identifiers.

### JQG-003 · Static checks (MUST)
Run the configured formatting, static-quality and security analyses. Fail on policy-defined blocking findings, not on the mere presence of informational warnings.
**Evidence:** scanner reports, rule-set versions and dispositions.

### JQG-004 · Automated tests (MUST)
Execute required unit and applicable integration tests. Fail when mandatory tests fail or required test evidence is missing.
**Evidence:** machine-readable test reports.

### JQG-005 · Dependency and secret checks (MUST)
Run configured dependency-vulnerability and secret scans. Evaluate findings according to defined severity and exception policy.
**Evidence:** scan results and decisions.

### JQG-006 · Traceability (MUST)
Associate gate outcome with source revision, policy versions, tool versions, applied exceptions, reports and resulting artifacts.
**Evidence:** gate manifest.

### JQG-007 · Exceptions (MUST)
Every exception requires identifier, affected rule, scope, rationale, approving authority, evidence and expiration/review condition. An exception must never silently bypass a gate.
**Evidence:** approved exception record.

## Outcomes
`PASS`: all mandatory controls satisfied. `FAIL`: at least one blocking control failed. `EXCEPTION`: a blocking condition has been formally accepted under the configured exception process; acceptance criteria must be explicit. `NOT_APPLICABLE`: the control does not apply, with rationale.

## Project-specific thresholds
Severity cutoffs, complexity ceilings, coverage percentages and supported-platform matrices are **not** invented here. They are supplied by applicable corporate/departmental policy or project configuration.

## Typical tools (not mandatory)
SonarQube, Java compiler/build tools, test runners, dependency scanners and secret scanners. The contract must not be tied to a vendor.
